 Suppose $\mathbf{v} = \begin{pmatrix} v_1 \\ v_2 \\ v_3 \end{pmatrix}$ and $\mathbf{w} = \begin{pmatrix} w_1 \\ w_2 \\ w_3 \end{pmatrix}.$  We can write
\[\mathbf{v} = \begin{pmatrix} v_1 \\ v_2 \\ v_3 \end{pmatrix} = k \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ -3 \end{pmatrix} + \begin{pmatrix} v_1 - k \\ v_2 \\ v_3 + 3k \end{pmatrix}.\]We can evaluate coordinates in terms of $v_1, v_2, v_3,$ and $k.$  (Is it easy to find such a vector value for $k?$)

[asy]
unitsize(1 cm);

pair A, B, O, P, Q;

O = (0,0);
P = (1,2);
Q = (4,6);
A = (2*Q + (4*P)/5)/3;
B = (3*Q + (4*P)/5)/3;

CD(O,P,-4,2);
CD(P,Q,4,2);
CD(O,B,-4,2);

label("$\mathbf{v}$", P, E, UnFill);
label("$\mathbf{w}$", Q, S, UnFill);
label("$\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ -3 \end{pmatrix}$", A, NW, UnFill);
label("$-2 \mathbf{v}$", B, NE, UnFill);
[/asy]

Finally,
\begin{align*}
\operatorname{proj}_{\mathbf{w}} (-2 \mathbf{v}) &= \operatorname{proj}_{\mathbf{w}} \left( -2 k \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ -3 \end{pmatrix} + \begin{pmatrix} -2 (v_1 - k) \\ -2 v_2 \\ -2 (v_3 + 3k) \end{pmatrix} \right) \\
&= \operatorname{proj}_{\mathbf{w}} (-2 k \mathbf{w}) + \operatorname{proj}_{\mathbf{w}} \left( \begin{pmatrix} -2 (v_1 - k) \\ -2 v_2 \\ -2 (v_3 + 3k) \end{pmatrix} \right).
\end{align*}As $\operatorname{proj}_{\mathbf{w}} (\alpha \mathbf{w}) = \alpha \operatorname{proj}_{\mathbf{w}} (\mathbf{w}) = \alpha \mathbf{w},$ the first term is just
\[-2k \operatorname{proj}_{\mathbf{w}} \mathbf{w} = -2k \mathbf{w}.\]The second term can be written as $\begin{pmatrix} -2 (v_1 - k) \\ -2 v_2 \\ -2 (v_3 + 3k) \end{pmatrix},$ so $\operatorname{proj}_{\mathbf{w}} (-2 \mathbf{v}) = \boxed{\begin{pmatrix} -2 (v_1 - k) \\ -2 v_2 \\ -2 (v_3 + 3k) \end{pmatrix}}.$ </think> <answer> \begin{pmatrix} -2 (v_1 - k) \\ -2 v_2 \\ -2 (v_3 + 3k) \end{pmatrix} </answer>

